/* size.sym -- SIZES OF ROUTINES THE GAME COPIES INTO RAM AND RUNS THERE.
 *
 * WHY THIS FILE EXISTS, and why these are not const.sym entries.
 *
 * A few functions allocate a scratch buffer, DMA the bytes of an ARM routine
 * into it, call it there, and free the buffer. The byte count is the SIZE of
 * that routine -- the distance to the next symbol -- so in the original source
 * it was a link-time value, not a literal. gcc-2.96 therefore has no constant
 * to build and emits a pool load, which is what the disassembly shows:
 *
 *     ldr r5, =0x278        rather than   mov r5, #0x9e / lsl r5, #2
 *     ldr r5, =0x9c         rather than   mov r5, #0x9c
 *     ldr r5, =0x7c         rather than   mov r5, #0x7c
 *
 * The last two are the strongest form of the tell const.sym's header
 * describes: an eight-bit `mov` builds either one, and the ROM pools it anyway.
 *
 * THE VALUES ARE VERIFIED ARITHMETIC, not guesses. asm/rom_15000/rom_15430.s
 * holds three consecutive ARM routines, and each size is the gap to the next:
 *
 *     Func_8015afc @ 0x08015afc   size 0x278  -> Func_8015d74
 *     Func_8015d74 @ 0x08015d74   size 0x9c   -> Func_8015e10
 *     Func_8015e10 @ 0x08015e10   size 0x7c   -> 0x08015e8c, its .func_end
 *
 * WHY NOT WRITE THE SUBTRACTION IN C, which would need no symbols at all.
 * Measured: `(unsigned)(Func_8015d74 - Func_8015afc)` with both declared
 * `extern unsigned char []` emits TWO pool words and a runtime `sub`. gcc
 * cannot fold a difference of two external symbols, and the assembler never
 * gets the chance because the routines live in a separately assembled ARM
 * file. So a single symbol carrying the size is what reproduces the ROM, and
 * it is also what the original source must have had.
 *
 * CRITERION 2 for the first of them, measured on DecompressIcon with every
 * other instruction in the function already exact. Eight literal spellings --
 * `0x278`, `0x278u`, `0x27 * 0x10 + 8`, `(unsigned)(0x9e << 2)`,
 * `sizeof(int) * 158`, a `const unsigned` initialiser, an `unsigned short`
 * local and a `volatile` round-trip -- all emit `mov #0x9e / lsl #2`. The last
 * two are worse than the rest, costing two and four extra instructions.
 *
 * The ROM's own counterexample: it BUILDS 632 with `mov #0x9e / lsl #2` at four
 * unrelated sites (rom_7ac2d8, rom_7c7b9c, rom_7ddb88, rom_f4000) and POOLS it
 * only in the two functions that copy this decoder. Same value, same ROM, two
 * treatments, split along exactly the boundary the identification predicts.
 *
 * NAMED FOR THE ROUTINE MEASURED, which is the one thing established here. An
 * absolute assignment in a linker script emits no bytes, so a wrong name costs
 * nothing and a wrong value fails `make compare` at once. Both users --
 * DecompressIcon and LoadIcon -- match with these and the build is green.
 */

_SIZE_8015afc = 0x278;
_SIZE_8015d74 = 0x9c;
_SIZE_8015e10 = 0x7c;
